Add files and folders to an archive (zip, 7z, tar, cpio and ar, and tar and cpio under any
codec). Each input is stored under its own name, as `archive.create` stores them, in `dir` if
given; an ar holds files only. The archive
isn't written in place: a new one is written beside it and swapped in, so a failed or
cancelled add leaves it as it was. Entries that stay are copied without decoding. An archive
whose entries are encrypted needs a password for the new files, unless `unencrypted`.

Usage: beezip add [OPTIONS] <ARCHIVE> <INPUTS>...

Arguments:
  <ARCHIVE>
          The archive to add to

  <INPUTS>...
          Files and folders to add

Options:
      --dir <DIR>
          The folder inside the archive to add into [default: the top]

      --overwrite <OVERWRITE>
          What to do when the archive has an entry with a new file's path (ignoring case on
          Windows): `ask`, `always` (replace it), `never` (keep it), `newer` (replace it if the file
          is newer) or `rename` (add the file as `a (1).txt`) [default: from config, `ask`]

          Possible values:
          - ask:    Ask (in a terminal or the app); fail with `Conflict` when nobody can be asked
          - always
          - never
          - rename: Write `name (1).ext` instead
          - newer:  Replace only if the entry is newer

  -m, --method <METHOD>
          The compression method for the new files and any 7z block encoded again: for zip `store`,
          `deflate` (the default), `bzip2`, `zstd`, `xz` or `ppmd`; for 7z `lzma2` (the default),
          `lzma`, `ppmd`, `bzip2`, `deflate` or `copy`. A tar keeps its codec
          
          [possible values: store, deflate, bzip2, zstd, xz, ppmd, lzma2, lzma, copy, mszip, gzip,
          lz4, lzo]

  -l, --level <LEVEL>
          The compression level, for the new files, or a compressed tar's stream

      --threads <THREADS>
          Compression threads for codecs that have them; 0 is one per core [default: from config]

  -p, --password <PASSWORD>
          Encrypt the new files with this password (zip, 7z). It also reads a 7z whose names are
          encrypted

      --encryption <ENCRYPTION>
          How to encrypt a zip's new files [default: aes256]. Zip only

          Possible values:
          - aes256:    AES-256, the default
          - zipcrypto: The legacy scheme, for tools such as Windows Explorer that can't read AES.
            Weak

      --unencrypted[=<UNENCRYPTED>]
          Add the new files in the clear to an archive whose entries are encrypted
          
          [possible values: true, false]

      --solid[=<SOLID>]
          Compress the new files together in solid blocks [default: true]. 7z only
          
          [possible values: true, false]

      --exclude <PATTERN>
          Leave out files and folders matching these patterns

  -L, --follow-links[=<FOLLOW_LINKS>]
          Store what symlinks point to instead of the links
          
          [possible values: true, false]

      --name-encoding <ENCODING>
          How names stored as bytes are read, so `dir` matches them as listed [default: from config,
          `auto`]
          
          [possible values: auto, utf-8, cp437, cp850, cp852, cp866, cp874, cp932, cp936, cp949,
          cp950, cp1250, cp1251, cp1252, cp1253, cp1254, cp1255, cp1256, cp1257, cp1258, koi8-r,
          koi8-u, iso-8859-1, iso-8859-2, iso-8859-5, iso-8859-7, euc-jp, gb18030]

      --dry-run[=<DRY_RUN>]
          Show what would change without writing anything
          
          [possible values: true, false]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

Global options:
      --output <FORMAT>
          How to print results; ndjson streams every job event

          Possible values:
          - human:  Text for people
          - json:   One JSON document on stdout: the output, or `{"error": {...}}`
          - ndjson: Every job event as a JSON line on stdout, ending with `Finished`, `Failed` or
            `Cancelled`
          
          [default: human]

  -q, --quiet
          No progress bars or info messages; warnings still print
